RETAIL-DERIVED FACTS

A bounded class of retail-derived facts is carried in the source itself:
format and asset identifiers, enumeration names, the executable's audio
bank-name catalog (whose authoritative copy is
../Trailmap/specs/data/external-sound-banks-v1.json), and the per-course race
timing constants -- lap counts and showoff clock lengths read from the
executable's own tables -- in Bundle/RaceBundle.cs. These are the functional
interface the extractor interoperates through, retained because interoperating
requires them. Beyond that interface, individual measured engine constants and
per-asset measurements appear in the source and tests where a behaviour had to
be matched -- a selector remap table, the event ids an engine routine compares
against, a course bank's slot sizes, a staging-formation centroid -- each cited
to the Trailmap chapter that establishes it. No retail asset, disc image, or
executable accompanies any of this, and everything else is generated locally
from a user-supplied disc image and stays out of version control.

One further class is worth naming exactly, because it concerns instruction
shape rather than names. Services/SoundIndexService.cs finds the collision-sound
dispatcher and resolver in *your* boot executable by matching the shape of a
compiler-emitted jump table. It describes each instruction by its opcode,
function and shift fields and by which register fields must agree with one
another; every operand that carries data -- the table address, the case count,
the branch targets -- is read from your file rather than compared against a
constant, the one fixed assumption being the register pair (s0, s1) the
resolver stores its two results through, and the source carries no encoded
instruction at all. It is used as a search key, the way a file format is found
by its magic number, and it is not a reproduction of the routine it locates.

