EXECUTABLE PATCHES

The patch definitions in Patches/ modify a game executable inside a user-supplied
disc image. They do not reproduce the retail bytes they replace: each region
names only a SHA-256 digest of the bytes it expects to
find, so applying a patch verifies before it writes, and reverting replays the
originals saved from the user's own image at apply time.

A trampoline hook has to re-execute whatever instruction its branch displaced,
and that instruction is the game's. Rather than carry it, a region names a
"graft": where in the user's own executable to copy those bytes from at apply
time. The six regions that need one cover 36 bytes across four regions in
noclip and 8 across two in sky-color; until apply fills them they read as MIPS
`break`. So no patch
definition in this directory contains a retail instruction, and neither do the
tools that author them under Trailmap/tools/patches/: all three recognise a build
and verify its hook sites by digest rather than carrying the words at those sites.

The research probes outside that path -- Trailmap/tools/instrumentation/ and
its PINE hooks -- follow the same rule: each site they attach to is named by the
SHA-256 of the words it holds, the words themselves are read from the running
emulator and checked against that digest at install, and a site that is already
detoured is restored from the operator's own extracted executable. They patch a
running emulator's memory rather than a disc, and what they write -- capture
data, telemetry, and the recovery record an uninstall replays -- stays on your
own machine; nothing they contain reaches a disc image.

That digest is there to verify, not to conceal, and it should not be read as
one. A hash is a one-way commitment to its input. Most regions are a single
instruction or flag word, and for an input that small the space of possible
originals is easy to search, so such a digest does in practice identify the
word it stands for; this is a property of hashing any tiny input rather than of
this file format. The noclip hook body instead overwrites a span of dead code
in the executable, and its regions digest that span whole (the largest is 432
bytes) so that apply and revert can verify it; those digests are not
practically invertible. No patch file carries the bytes of any region it
replaces.

The sky-color patch installs a 13-entry, 3-bytes-per-entry RGB table that its
hook indexes at load time. On first application Snowknife reconstructs those
entries from constant stores in the supported executable's own WorldConf
initializer and verifies the result against a digest. The public definition
carries the extraction recipe and digest, not the 39 retail channel values;
setting one course's sky still leaves the other twelve visually unchanged.

